menu "Station: diagnostics"

    config DIAG_LOG_RING_KB
        int "Log ring for the web UI (KB per boot, PSRAM, kept across resets)"
        range 8 1024
        default 64
        help
            Every log line is also copied here so it can be read remotely
            (web UI log view, WebSocket stream). The ring survives software,
            panic and watchdog resets, so the previous boot's last lines can
            be read after a crash: two rings of this size are kept in PSRAM,
            this boot's and the previous boot's.

    config DIAG_LOG_LINE_MAX
        int "Longest log line kept (bytes)"
        range 128 1024
        default 256
        help
            Each line is formatted on the stack of the task that logs, so
            this adds to every logging task's stack use. Longer lines are
            cut and end in "..." on the console and in the log ring.

    config DIAG_CONSOLE_TX_BUF
        int "Console transmit buffer (bytes, internal RAM)"
        range 1024 32768
        default 4096
        help
            The serial console runs at 115200 baud (about 11.5 KB/s). A log
            line goes straight into this buffer only when nothing older waits
            and the whole line fits, so a burst never stalls the task that
            logs. Otherwise the line waits in the log ring and the console
            task sends it as the buffer drains, in order. A line is lost only
            when the log ring overwrote it before it was sent; the console
            then says how many, on a line of its own.

    config DIAG_CONSOLE_TASK_STACK
        int "Console task stack (bytes, PSRAM)"
        range 2048 8192
        default 2560
        help
            The low-priority task that copies waiting log lines from the log
            ring to the console. It formats one short note and calls the
            UART driver; it never logs itself. Its stack is in PSRAM: the
            task only copies lines into the UART driver's transmit buffer
            and does no flash or NVS work (a PSRAM stack must never be in
            use while the cache is off). Only when PSRAM cannot provide the
            stack is it taken from internal RAM, and the boot log says so.

    config DIAG_CONSOLE_USB_MIRROR
        bool "Copy the console to the built-in USB (USB Serial/JTAG)"
        default n
        depends on SOC_USB_SERIAL_JTAG_SUPPORTED && ESP_CONSOLE_UART
        select USJ_ENABLE_USB_SERIAL_JTAG
        select ESP_PHY_ENABLE_USB if SOC_WIFI_PHY_NEEDS_USB_WORKAROUND
        help
            The station board's ESP32S3 USB-C port reaches the UART0
            console's USB bridge (CH343P) with the plug one way round and the
            chip's built-in USB Serial/JTAG the other way round. With this
            option the console's output (log lines, the bench console's
            replies) also goes out on the built-in USB, so either way round
            shows the log, and the built-in USB stays on while the station
            runs, so esptool can reset the chip into download mode through
            it. Console input stays on UART0 only.

            The copy never holds up the console: each write goes to the USB
            port's transmit buffer whole or not at all, without waiting.
            Without a host on that port nothing is sent; with one that does
            not read, what does not fit is dropped and counted (GET
            /api/health, log.usb_dropped), and the next line sent there says
            how many. The copy starts with diag, the first thing the app
            does: the app's startup lines before it and panic output are not
            copied.

            Off by default; bench builds turn it on (sdkconfig.bench). It
            keeps the USB PHY powered while Wi-Fi runs, with or without a
            host on the port (it selects ESP_PHY_ENABLE_USB and
            USJ_ENABLE_USB_SERIAL_JTAG), and the ESP-IDF help says that can
            lower Wi-Fi performance. How much on the station board has not
            been measured: compare the signal strength and throughput of one
            board with and without it before turning it on for a station.

            Turning it off in a build directory that had it on leaves the
            other two on there: that sdkconfig still says
            CONFIG_ESP_PHY_ENABLE_USB=y and
            CONFIG_USJ_ENABLE_USB_SERIAL_JTAG=y. Set all three to n in it
            (menuconfig, or edit the file):
                CONFIG_DIAG_CONSOLE_USB_MIRROR=n
                CONFIG_USJ_ENABLE_USB_SERIAL_JTAG=n
                CONFIG_ESP_PHY_ENABLE_USB=n
            or start a new build directory, which takes them from
            sdkconfig.defaults. The configure step warns when the USB PHY is
            on outside a bench build, or without this option.

    config DIAG_CONSOLE_USB_TX_BUF
        int "Built-in USB transmit buffer (bytes, internal RAM)"
        depends on DIAG_CONSOLE_USB_MIRROR
        range 512 16384
        default 4096
        help
            Room for what the console copies to the built-in USB while the
            host has not read it yet. The copy gets what the UART gets, as
            the UART's transmit buffer takes it, so the same size as that
            buffer (DIAG_CONSOLE_TX_BUF) holds a full burst while the host
            reads at USB speed.

    config DIAG_JOURNAL_MIN_INTERVAL_MS
        int "Minimum interval between journal records of one kind (ms)"
        range 0 60000
        default 1000
        help
            Rate limit that keeps a flapping condition from wearing out the
            journal partition. A record it refuses is reported as not
            written, so callers that accumulate deltas keep them pending.
            Health-ladder step records are exempt: one step's result and the
            next step's start fall in the same second, and the ladder bounds
            their rate itself (each step has a grace of at least 5 s).

    config DIAG_JOURNAL_BUDGET_PER_H
        int "Journal records per hour, all kinds together"
        range 60 36000
        default 1800
        help
            Second rate limit, over all kinds: at most this many records per
            hour on average, of which up to ten minutes' worth may come at
            once. Refused records are reported like the per-kind limit's;
            ladder step records are exempt. Every 127 records cost one
            sector erase: at the default, a journal written at the full rate
            without a break would take more than 20 years to reach 100 000
            erases per sector.

endmenu
